---
<QuestionLayout
  title="Is the agent MCP exact-ref demo the same as traverse-mcp?"
  description="No. The Oct 9 demo is an in-process MCP-shaped façade over ExactModelBrowserHost under Node. Mode A traverse-mcp is the published stdio server for registry capabilities. Pinned binaries (#1653) ship with the next product tag."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/is-the-agent-mcp-demo-the-same-as-traverse-mcp.html"
  category="AI and MCP"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p><strong>No.</strong> The October 9 weekly demo and Mode A <code>traverse-mcp</code> both speak something an agent can call. They are not the same program.</p>

  <h2>What the demo is</h2>
  <p>The write-up <a href="/blog/agent-mcp-exact-ref.html">Agent speaks MCP. Exact pin kills the freestyle.</a> shows an agent calling MCP-shaped tools against a signed exact-ref package. Behind those tools is an <strong>in-process JSON-RPC façade</strong> over <code>ExactModelBrowserHost</code> from <code>traverse-embedder-web@0.14.0</code>, running under Node. The host is the published web exact-ref connector. The façade is demo code for that week. It is not a binary you pin from a release.</p>

  <h2>What traverse-mcp is</h2>
  <p>Mode A <code>traverse-mcp</code> is the published stdio MCP server. An agent uses it to discover, inspect, and execute registry capabilities: <code>list_entrypoints</code>, <code>describe_entrypoint</code>, <code>execute_entrypoint</code>, and the rest of the ten-command set on <a href="/agents.html">For AI agents</a>. Setup is the <a href="/docs/guides/mcp-setup.html">MCP setup guide</a>. Stdio is the packaged bootstrap. That server is a client of the shared runtime contract. It is not the demo façade, and it is not a second runtime.</p>

  <h2>What is still open</h2>
  <p>Pinning and checksum-verifying a packaged Mode A binary, the way you would pin any other release artifact, is <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1653">#1653</a>. The packaging workflow merged on main on October 15 (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1701">#1701</a>). It builds Linux x86_64 and macOS arm64 binaries on the next product tag and publishes them as a non-Latest <code>traverse-mcp-v*</code> release with <code>SHA256SUMS</code> and build provenance. No such release exists yet. Until one does, do not describe the October 9 column as "the checksum-pinned <code>traverse-mcp</code>." Quote the blog for what the façade did. Quote this page, or <a href="/agents.html">agents</a>, for what the server is.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
